# The Drowned Quarter

The oldest quarter in Saltmere and the one the sea takes first. It was
built on pilings and lime-brick in the years when the marsh was dry
enough to be worth building on, and then the sea came back, and the
quarter learned to live with the tide instead of arguing with it.

Everything here stands a little lower than everywhere else. Doorsteps are
broad and shallow. Roofs pitch the wrong way, to shed rain into the
street rather than the lane. In a spring tide the gutters run backwards,
and the fish-market boys tie their crates to the door rings and go
indoors, and the market reopens when the water goes down.

The tall salt-pans are at the northern edge, on the flats the marsh keeps
dry by holding its breath. Below them the quarter falls away into the
drowned part proper: streets that are canals at half-tide, houses with a
ground floor and a boat door and not much in between.

The fish-market is at the low end. The tide-house is at the high end.
Between them runs a lane called **Sallow Lane**, which smells of nothing
at all, which in Saltmere means it is the most expensive street in the
city.

The street ends at a wall of salt-block with a door in it that the tide
does not reach.

> [!secret] The north door
> The drowned door in the north wall is not a collapsed cellar. It is a
> customs door: the Crown has taxed the marsh itself for forty years, and
> every unwatched tide has run salt through it. Hessa Cray told the party
> this in the fish-market, and they did not believe her until the tide came
> up over the sill and a lighter went through without stopping. ^saltgate

> [!secret] The salt-line
> The bloom on the Causeway water ran a mile before it went, and it left a
> white line on the tide wall that is still there, because nobody has
> scrubbed it and nobody will. It is the only piece of evidence of what
> was in the crate, and it will still be there at the spring tide. ^saltline
